    text: Ms. Lineman notes that voters have already rejected turf once and requests
      the board provide more information on organic options and delay the vote until
      November for greater turnout. She also questions the financial wisdom of a long-term
      bond for a product with a 10-year lifespan.
  - seconds: 4171
    speaker: James Lawler
    text: Mr. Lawler asks if the district has reached out to local industries and
      large businesses in South Portland, such as the semiconductor industry or Dick's
      Sporting Goods, to help fund the project.
  - seconds: 4202
    speaker: Gabriel Harp
    text: Mr. Harp speaks in favor of the synthetic turf option, arguing it vastly
      expands athletic time for children, which he sees as a life-changing benefit.
      He believes the consultant is neutral and that a single organic grass field
      cannot withstand the volume of athletic activity the community needs.
  - seconds: 4373
    speaker: Sarah Lian
    text: A student at SPHS, Ms. Lian argues that the current field has not 'lasted'
      58 years, describing it as unsafe with bare patches of mud. She also notes that
      while Wainwright fields are better, they are not suitable for games due to a
      lack of bleachers and the presence of sprinkler heads on the playing surface.
  - seconds: 4565
    speaker: Jana Grant
    text: Ms. Grant speaks in favor of synthetic turf, highlighting the increased
      accessibility it provides. She clarifies that modern turf systems do not use
      the crumb rubber that gets tracked everywhere and notes that comparing South
      Portland to Springfield, MA is difficult given Springfield's much larger population
      and tax base.
  - seconds: 4734
    speaker: Eleni Richardson
    text: Ms. Richardson supports synthetic turf but calls for a clearer 'marketing
      campaign' to address community concerns and explain how the district arrived
      at its proposal. She believes the previous referendum failed due to cost, not
      an attack on turf, and that the district has good answers for the environmental
      issues being raised.
  - seconds: 4845
    speaker: Andrew Greeley
    text: Mr. Greeley supports the enhancements in general, preferably with synthetic
      turf for durability. He notes the bond covers the entire package, not just the
      turf, and argues that if a grass proposal moves forward, it must include the
      full, realistic cost of the intensive maintenance required.
